# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout rewrites CRLF into every tracked file and
# `git status` reports the whole tree as modified with zero content change
# (8,290 insertions against 8,290 deletions, all whitespace). Git now stores
# LF in the repository and checks out whatever the platform wants.
#
# After adding this file, run once to fix the history-to-worktree mismatch:
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "Normalise line endings"

* text=auto

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.db    binary
*.snk   binary

# Scripts are format-sensitive: a .sh with CRLF fails on Linux with
# "bad interpreter", a .cmd with LF misbehaves on Windows.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
